UP|52|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|What happens if North opens 2@D. It actually might work out better for EW. East should then bid 2N showing 15-18 over a Weak-2. That ends up getting passed out. I lead a spade and they end up with a plus score.|
PG| ***** 21 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Another thing that West might have done, although I don't think it was correct with the hand was to double 2@C suggesting clubs as a contract. Then East could bid 3@C. |
PG| ***** 22 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Also if East opens 1@C, then West might make a preemptive 3@C bid and they end up in 3@C. In that auction they lose 3 hearts, 1 club, and 1 spade, and go down 1. |
PG| ***** 23 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|BTW, 1@C is the correct opening bid for East. So as you can see, there were many possible auctions depending on what each player decided to do.|
PG| ***** 24 ***** |


