UP|52|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Probably the best way to play the hand is to start with the @SA and @SK and then cash the @HA discarding a club. By doing so, West ends up taking 4 spades, 2 hearts, and 2 diamonds for 8 tricks. |
PG| ***** 88 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|It is often correct not draw trump immediately. So West had reasonable instincts. But you have to have a good reason not to draw trump, usually because you want to ruff something in dummy. On this hand there is no good reason to delay drawing trump.|
PG| ***** 89 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|As to the bidding, East probably should make a limit raise in spades by cue bidding. East has 2 cue bids available--2@D and 2@H. When that (rarely) occurs you can actually differentiate between a 3-card limit raise and 4-card limit raise. |
PG| ***** 90 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|If you make the lower cue bid it shows a 3-card raise and if you make the higher cue bid it shows a 4-card raise. So on this hand East could bid 2@D to show a 3-card limit raise in spades.|
PG| ***** 91 ***** |


